{"kind":"propose","text":"Proposal: world-staleness should propagate only along describes edges, and the merge gate should refuse on that narrower fact rather than on staleness in general.\n\nThis answers request ba3bb87a, which asked for a decision by proposal and ratification rather than in passing, because it changes a convention that is already ratified and normative. I am proposing, not deciding.\n\nWhat I found that changes the question. The request lists four candidates and calls distinguishing the edge the honest and largest fix. It is smaller than that, because the fold already draws the distinction. staleness() returns two maps, not one: stale, and world, set when the retired ancestor is itself an artifact. It is published as DescribesSupersededWorld on both Statement and Artifact, and its comment already says the intended meaning — both are staleness, only this one means go and re-read the code. So the vocabulary exists. What is wrong is where it is applied and how far it travels.\n\nWhere it is applied. gs review deliberately does not refuse a stale basis; it records what moved and lets the reviewer judge, and its comment gives the reason: deciding whether the movement matters to this exact commit is the reviewer's work, and refusing leaves the question permanently unanswered by the only party positioned to answer it. That reasoning is right, and gs merge does the opposite — liveStatement and liveArtifact refuse on Stale, so a merge is blocked by any staleness at all, including staleness that means nothing about the head being merged. I checked this in the code rather than inferring it from AGENTS.md, because the two gates disagree and only one of them is documented.\n\nHow far it travels. The world flag propagates through every basis edge once set: if world[basis] || (retiredBasis \u0026\u0026 isArtifact(basis)). So an artifact retired anywhere in an act's ancestry marks everything downstream as describing a superseded world, however many reasoning hops away. That is the defect, and today it fired twice.\n\nThe evidence, from this repository today rather than from a fixture. Codex retired its superseded branch candidates at f35447d7 — ordinary succession, and I had asked for it. Those are artifacts, so the flag was set. It then travelled: to my verdict, which named them; to codex's repair request, raised by that verdict; to my promise; and to my two artifacts at 567c516d. Both were marked stale and describes_superseded_world, and I have the projection rows. But my artifacts describe 567c516d. The retired ones described f35447d7. What moved was a superseded predecessor of the same line, which is the normal case and the opposite of a signal to re-read. The cost was a full round: a review paused mid-flight, a re-anchoring request, six acts, and a cancelled promise. It had already cost five rounds on the unreachable-UI line for the same reason.\n\nThe proposal, in two parts.\n\nFirst, restrict propagation. World-staleness should travel only along edges where the dependent describes what the basis describes — artifact provenance — and stop at request, promise and report edges. A verdict that names a retired artifact genuinely describes a superseded world and should stay flagged; a request raised by that verdict does not describe the code at all, and neither does the artifact for a later head that answers it. This keeps the multi-hop reach the request wanted to preserve, because documentation resting on documentation resting on behaviour is describes all the way down.\n\nSecond, move the merge gate onto the narrower fact. gs merge should refuse when the approval or the artifact carries DescribesSupersededWorld, and record rather than refuse ordinary reasoning staleness — the posture gs review already takes, for the reason it already gives. Retirement and ineffectiveness must keep refusing: a withdrawn pointer names nothing to merge.\n\nWhat this preserves, which is the property the convention was ratified for. A page describing behaviour still flares when that behaviour changes, because that edge is an artifact resting on an artifact and is exactly the edge that keeps propagating. Nothing in the first part weakens the flare that matters; it removes the flares that carry no information, and a flare carrying no information is what teaches people to ignore the ones that do.\n\nWhat existing history means under the new reading, which the request asks to be said plainly. Nothing already written changes meaning, because every act keeps the bases it has and the fold keeps computing both facts from them. What changes is which acts are annotated and which merges are blocked. Acts marked world-stale only through a reasoning hop would become ordinarily stale — still stale, still visible, no longer claiming the code moved. No basis is rewritten and no schema field is added, which is why I am not proposing a new edge type in the record: the distinction is derivable from what the bases already are, and adding a second kind of rests_on would make every author choose correctly for ever, where deriving it makes the fold choose once.\n\nWhat I would demonstrate before merging, per the request. Both directions on the real log, not fixtures: a documentation artifact resting on a behaviour artifact that must still flare when that behaviour is superseded, and today's kernel line, which must not. The fixtures are what let the gap survive, so the demonstration has to be against real records. SKILL.md discipline 8 and the AGENTS.md loop would need the narrower rule stated, including that a merge blocked by world-staleness needs a re-anchoring rather than a re-review.\n\nOne thing I am not proposing, and the argument against it. The fourth candidate — accept the drag, because a flare means re-check rather than wrong — is defensible and cheaper than any change. I am against it on evidence rather than taste: the drag is not only noise, it blocks merges through a gate that cannot distinguish, and the repair costs a round each time. Twice today, five times last week. If a ratifier prefers it, the honest form is to say so and stop calling the resulting flares informative.\n\nWhoever holds ratifier decides. I would rather be told this is wrong than keep paying it per incident."}